beyond B with BF′=DF and AF′=AF. The 45° condition gives ∠EAF′=∠EAF, so SAS yields EF=EF′=BE+DF.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_247_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"quarter-turn-congruence","label":"Rotate ADF and apply SAS","nodeIds":["cnm_247","cnm_245","cnm_123","cnm_121"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_055"],"id":"solution:quarter-turn-congruence:0d781cd76e3c","label":"Rotate ADF and compare triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quarter-turn-congruence":["cnm_247","cnm_245","cnm_123","cnm_121"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_247","cnm_245","cnm_123","cnm_121","cnm_058","cnm_055"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"150°","reason":"Rotate about B through 60° taking A to C and P to P′. Then CP′=3, PP′=4, and CP=5, so ∠CP′P=90°. Triangle BPP′ is equilateral, and the interior configuration gives ∠APB=∠CP′B=90°+60°=150°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_247_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_245","cnm_115"],"id":"sixty-degree-rotation","label":"Rotate to construct a right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sixty-degree-rotation":["cnm_247","cnm_177","cnm_058"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_247","cnm_177","cnm_058","cnm_245","cnm_115"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, √7","reason":"Place A=(0,0), B=(s,0), C=(s/2,√3s/2), and let u=s². Subtracting distance equations gives P=((u−2)/(2s),(u−4)/(2√3s)). AP²=1 then yields u²−8u+7=0. The root u=1 puts P outside; u=7 gives interior barycentric coordinates (4/7,2/7,1/7). The distance differences uniquely determine P, so s=√7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174"],"id":"sixty-degree-rota